Rebased copy of `03-commit-version-check.patch` against alchemy `main` at 3ff34b94f6c4 (2026-11-17).
Apply from the repository root with `git apply`; the text above the first `diff --git` line is
ignored. The original, against 33c10dd, is `../03-commit-version-check.patch`.

## State of this copy

- Formatted with the repository's `oxfmt`; `oxfmt --check`, `oxlint` and `pnpm typecheck` pass
  with the whole set applied.
- Carries its tests: `packages/integration-tests/test/Domain/CommitVersionCheck.test.ts`, with a
  two-runner in-process host (`two-runner-host.ts`) and its model (`two-runner-model.ts`). Both
  cases fail on `main` and pass with this patch; the held-instance case also fails with the version
  check alone.

## What changed from the original

- `main` already imports `effect/Data` in `DurableObjects.ts`, so the import hunk is gone.
- `main` split the commit into `commit` and `persist` (#3373, #3420): the receipt is written first
  inside the transaction and `persist` writes the events, `meta`, the routed marks and the outbox.
  The version check is now the first statement of that transaction, before the receipt.
- New on `main`: a decision (#3420) can hold an instance (`meta.held`) between its read and its
  commit. The check also refuses a commit when the stored `meta` is held, and the refusal is then
  `InstanceHeld`, which `whenFree` already settles and retries, instead of `CommitConflict`. This
  is an addition to what ran on a cluster: it has run in the in-process test only.
- A refused commit fails with `yield* new CommitConflict(...)` rather than `Effect.fail(...)`, as
  the repository's lint asks.
- The Effect Cluster hunk (`pg_advisory_xact_lock` in `objectStorage.transaction`) is unchanged.

The original description follows unchanged. Its line references are to 33c10dd.

fix(domain): never acknowledge a commit on a version that moved since decide

Against alchemy `packages/domain/src` at 33c10dd (2.0.0-beta.87). Apply from the repository root
with `git apply`; the text above the first `---` line is ignored.

## Problem

An aggregate's `commit` reads `meta`, runs `decide` on that state, and writes the events, `meta`,
the receipt and the outbox in one storage transaction. It does not check, inside that
transaction, that `meta` is still the version `decide` was given. It relies on one-at-a-time
execution per instance.

On Effect Cluster that holds only while one runner owns the instance. A runner that stalls (a GC
pause, a partition, a frozen container) past its shard lock's 35 s expiry keeps running the entity
it had; another runner takes the shard and serves the same instance. Both can then commit:

- the second runner decides on version 1 and commits version 2;
- the stalled runner's commit, decided on version 1 too, then writes `event:000000000002` and
  `meta` over it.

PostgreSQL row locks do not prevent this: both transactions read `meta` at version 1 before either
commits, and the second simply waits for the first's row locks and then overwrites. The first
write was acknowledged and is lost.

## Fix

- `commit`: the transaction first reads `meta` again and returns `undefined`, writing nothing, when
  its version is not the one `decide` was given. The commit then fails with a new `CommitConflict`
  (`id`, `decided`, `stored`).
- The object method `send` passes `CommitConflict` through as a failure of the call
  (`reportDefectsUnlessConflict`), where every other cause is still reported as a defect value. The
  caller's `unwrapDefects` already retries a failed call with `TRANSIT_RETRY`, under the same
  command id; a scheduled command's job is retried by its callback.
- Effect Cluster: `objectStorage.transaction` takes `pg_advisory_xact_lock(hashtextextended(object,
  0))` first, so transactions on one instance serialize across runners and the version check sees
  the other's commit. The lock ends with the transaction.

## Why it is safe

- Never acknowledged: a commit that loses the check writes nothing (the check is the transaction's
  first step) and returns no receipt.
- Idempotency: the retry starts from the receipt lookup, which answers first, as before. If the
  other runner committed this same command, the retry gets that receipt; otherwise it decides again
  on the stored state.
- One at a time: the retry is a new call, routed to whichever runner owns the instance now, so only
  the current owner decides again.
- Durable Objects (Cloudflare) and Rivet: storage transactions on one instance already serialize, so
  the check always passes. Its cost is one `meta` read inside the transaction.
- The in-memory platform has its own commit path and is unchanged.

One `meta` read and one advisory lock per commit on Effect Cluster. The advisory key is a 64-bit
hash of the object name, so two objects can share a key and serialize their commits; they never
deadlock, since a transaction takes one key.

The check orders commits by their transactions, not by shard ownership: when the stale runner's
transaction started first, the stale runner is acknowledged and the other runner's commit is the
one refused and retried. Nothing acknowledged is lost either way. Refusing the stale runner itself
needs a fence on shard ownership (`07-commit-fenced-on-shard-ownership.patch`).

## Failing case, and how it was reproduced

- In process (two in-process runners sharing one instance's storage, with serialized transactions
  and a `TestClock`): runner 0 decides on version 0 and holds; runner 1 commits version 1; runner
  0's commit returns. Before the change, runner 0 is acknowledged at version 1 and the stored events
  are `[A]` (B lost). After it, runner 0's first commit is refused, its retry is acknowledged at
  version 2, and the events are `[B, A]`.
- On a cluster (two runners and a front on Effect Cluster with PostgreSQL 17, on a single-node Docker
swarm on one workstation): a runner frozen with `docker pause` while its commit waited on a row lock
  held from another session, until the other runner had taken its shard. Before the change, in two
  runs, the frozen runner was acknowledged at version 2 and the other runner's event then replaced
  its own. After it, three runs lost no acknowledged write; the frozen runner was still
  acknowledged in all three, which 07 addresses.

## Where it ran

- On a cluster: the paused-inside-commit case above, and the Domain conformance suite in
  `packages/integration-tests/test/Domain/conformance/`, unchanged.
- In process: the two-runner case above; the in-memory and Durable Objects suites pass unchanged.

## Depends on

Nothing. No hunk overlaps 01, 02 or 06; 07 builds on this one.
